<Prose title="Privacy" description="What Warble and this website collect, which is nothing." image="/og/privacy.png" imageAlt="Warble's privacy: no account, no analytics or cookies, and what's playing stays on your Mac.">
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="lede">What Warble and this website collect, which is nothing.</p>
  <p>Warble doesn't collect anything about you. It reads what's playing on your Mac so it can show it in the notch, and that information stays on your Mac.</p>
  <p>Warble goes online to check GitHub for updates, and it asks before it does that.</p>
  <p>For a song playing in a browser, Warble searches Apple's iTunes Search for the album cover, since a browser only reports a frame of the video. The search carries the song's title and artist and nothing that identifies you, and a cover it finds is downloaded from Apple. Songs in other apps aren't searched for.</p>
  <p>When you click the artist on the open notch, Warble reads the song's page on Spotify's website for a Spotify song, or asks YouTube for the channel of a YouTube video. If Warble can't read what's playing from macOS and asks Music or Spotify directly instead, it downloads a Spotify song's cover from the address Spotify gives it. None of these requests carries anything that identifies you.</p>
  <p>This website has no cookies or analytics. Cloudflare hosts it and GitHub hosts the downloads, so <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's</a> and <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's</a> privacy policies cover those requests.</p>
  <p>Questions go in <a href="https://github.com/LorcanChinnock/warble/issues">GitHub issues</a>. For anything private, such as a security problem, use <a href="https://github.com/LorcanChinnock/warble/security/advisories/new">GitHub's private reporting</a>. See the <a href="https://github.com/LorcanChinnock/warble/blob/main/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>.</p>
  <p class="small">Last updated 16 November 2026.</p>
</Prose>
